Part 1: KPI Definition & Management
Q1: What types of KPIs can we create in the system?
A: The system supports six distinct KPI types to handle any metric your organization needs to track:
Count / Number - For tracking quantities like "Number of customers acquired," "Units produced," or "Training sessions conducted." The system simply counts and compares actual versus target.
Percentage (%) - For tracking rates and ratios like "Customer satisfaction rate," "Project completion percentage," or "Budget utilization." The system handles percentages intelligently, automatically capping at 100% where appropriate.
Financial Amount - For monetary values like "Revenue," "Cost savings," or "Grant utilization." Handles currency formatting and supports both higher-is-better (revenue) and lower-is-better (costs) logic.
Yes / No Milestone - For binary completions like "Strategic plan approved," "Policy document published," or "Compliance certificate obtained." Returns 100% for completed, 0% for not completed.
Time / Turnaround - For time-based metrics like "Invoice processing time," "Customer response time," or "Project delivery days." Lower-is-better logic applies automatically.
Impact Score - For scaled ratings like "Beneficiary satisfaction (1-5)," "Employee engagement score (1-10)," or "Net Promoter Score."
Each KPI type has its own validation rules, display formatting, and calculation behavior, ensuring your metrics are represented accurately.
Q2: How do we set up KPIs? Is it complicated?
A: Setting up KPIs is straightforward with our guided KPI creation wizard.
You start by selecting a sector template - we provide pre-built templates for Commercial, NGO, Public Sector, and Consulting organizations. These come with common KPIs already configured, so you can get started immediately.
The wizard walks you through three simple steps:
Step 1 - Basic Information: Enter the KPI name, unique code, description, and select the KPI type from the six options. This step also sets the calculation logic (higher-is-better or lower-is-better) and measure type (cumulative YTD or non-cumulative period-only).
Step 2 - Target Settings: Define optional target ranges (minimum and maximum acceptable values), the unit of measurement, decimal places for display, and any custom formula if the standard calculation doesn't fit your needs.
Step 3 - Framework Assignment: Link the KPI to a framework, sector, category, and assign an owner and department.
You can also bulk import KPIs from Excel or CSV if you have many to set up at once. The system validates your data and provides clear error messages if anything needs correction.
Q3: Can we edit or delete KPIs after they're created?
A: Yes, but with safeguards to maintain data integrity.
You can edit most KPI properties at any time - name, description, target ranges, unit, and strategic objective. The system maintains a complete history of all changes, so you can see who changed what and when.
However, once KPIs have associated data (targets, actuals, scores), certain critical fields become locked to prevent data corruption:
•	KPI type (COUNT, PERCENTAGE, etc.)
•	Calculation logic (higher-is-better, lower-is-better)
•	Measure type (cumulative, non-cumulative)
If you need to change these, you have two options:
First, you can deactivate the existing KPI (which preserves its historical data) and create a new one with the correct settings. This is the recommended approach for audit trails.
Second, as an administrator, you can force-edit these fields, but the system will warn you that this may affect existing calculations and require score recalculation.
Deleting a KPI is possible but restricted. If a KPI has any associated data (targets, actuals, scores), you cannot delete it - you can only deactivate it. This ensures you never lose historical performance data.
