Part 4: Scoring & Analytics
Q14: How are scores calculated?
A: Scores are calculated using formulas you define when setting up the KPI.
Higher is Better (Revenue, Customers, Beneficiaries):
text
Score = (Actual ÷ Target) × 100
Example: Actual 989M, Target 1B = 98.9% score
Lower is Better (Costs, Debt, Processing Time):
text
Score = (Target ÷ Actual) × 100
Example: Target 20M, Actual 32M = 62.5% score
Cumulative vs Non-Cumulative:
•	Cumulative (YTD): Monthly actuals add up over the year. Example: Revenue accumulates each month.
•	Non-Cumulative: Only the current month matters. Example: Employee satisfaction score is measured fresh each month.
Weighted Scores - KPIs have weights that sum to 100% per user. The overall score is the weighted average of individual KPI scores.
Example Calculation:
•	KPI A: Score 95%, Weight 40% → Contribution: 38.0
•	KPI B: Score 85%, Weight 35% → Contribution: 29.75
•	KPI C: Score 70%, Weight 25% → Contribution: 17.5
•	Overall Score: 38.0 + 29.75 + 17.5 = 85.25%
All calculations are automatic. As soon as data is approved, scores update immediately.
Q15: What do the traffic light colors mean?
A: The traffic light system provides instant visual status:
🟢 Green - On Track (90% and above): Performance meets or exceeds expectations. Action: Monitor and sustain.
🟡 Yellow - At Risk (50% - 89%): Performance below target, requires attention. Action: Review and intervene.
🔴 Red - Off Track (Below 50%): Significant performance gap, immediate intervention needed. Action: Escalate and remediate.
Consecutive Red Alerts - If a KPI shows red for two consecutive months, the system automatically sends alerts to the supervisor and HR, triggering a performance review or PIP if needed.
Trend Indicators - The system also shows whether performance is improving, declining, or stable, helping you distinguish between a single bad month and a worrying trend.
Customizable Thresholds - While default thresholds (90/50) work for most organizations, you can customize them per sector or per KPI. NGOs might use 85/60 thresholds, while public sector might use 95/70 for stricter compliance.
The traffic light appears on dashboards, reports, and notifications, providing consistent visual communication across the system.
