Part 6: Performance Improvement Plans (PIPs)
Q23: How does the PIP system work?
A: The system includes a structured Performance Improvement Plan (PIP) module for underperforming employees.
Initiation: A manager or HR can initiate a PIP for any employee whose performance has been consistently below expectations (typically red status for 2+ consecutive months).
PIP Components:
•	Improvement Areas - Specific KPIs or competencies needing improvement
•	Targets - Realistic, time-bound improvement goals
•	Action Plan - Step-by-step activities the employee must complete
•	Review Milestones - Checkpoints (weekly, bi-weekly) to assess progress
•	Coaching Notes - Manager observations and feedback during the PIP period
Workflow:
1.	Manager creates PIP with defined targets and timeline
2.	Employee acknowledges receipt and commits to the plan
3.	Regular review meetings are scheduled
4.	Progress is tracked against improvement targets
5.	At the end of PIP period, outcome is documented:
o	Successful - Employee met targets, PIP closed
o	Extended - Progress made but needs more time
o	Escalated - No improvement, refer to HR for further action
Automated Alerts:
•	PIP initiation notifications to employee and manager
•	Reminders for upcoming review meetings
•	Alerts when PIP timeline is approaching expiry
•	Notifications when targets are met
All PIPs are tracked in the system, providing a complete history of performance improvement efforts for HR documentation.
Q24: What happens when a PIP is successful vs unsuccessful?
A: The system tracks PIP outcomes with clear workflows.
Successful PIP:
•	Employee meets or exceeds improvement targets
•	Manager documents successful completion
•	System closes the PIP and archives the record
•	Employee receives confirmation notification
•	Performance monitoring continues through regular KPIs
Unsuccessful PIP:
•	Employee fails to meet improvement targets
•	Manager documents specific shortfalls
•	System escalates to HR or senior management
•	Options include: extended PIP, performance review, role change, or termination
•	Complete documentation supports any employment action
Partial Success:
•	Employee shows improvement but hasn't met all targets
•	Manager can extend PIP for additional period
•	Targets may be adjusted based on progress
•	System tracks extension history
All PIP records are immutable and available for future reference, providing legal protection and documentation of performance management efforts.
